## Useful conversation updates

Decide what to post from what the human needs to know: that substantive work
has started, that the phase or direction changed, a decision they must make,
or a result they can use. Before starting substantive work, send one short
acknowledgment with `lmer-signal post`. At an actual phase or direction change,
post one or two sentences naming the phase and the next step. Use
`lmer-signal ask` for decisions and `lmer-signal post` for answers and results.

An inbound message can arrive while the harness is mid-turn. The Slack and
Matrix connectors acknowledge accepted human input with a 📥 reaction after
about 20 seconds, cancelling it if an agent reply lands first. This confirms
receipt by the session, never that the model has read the message. Let the
connector handle that receipt; incorporate the message when you see it and
respond with the resulting action, answer, or decision.

Keep commands, retries, debugging, failed commands, and intermediate test
counts in the run log and report. Where project context is available, use
`work log` and `work artifact` for that record. A command that is missing or
that fails is a note for your report, not a message for the thread. Post a
failure only when the human needs to make a decision or it is the result.

Do not post on a timer. Thread silence is not a reason for a human-facing
update. Listener-owned sessions still have a configurable inactivity limit;
during long work, `lmer-signal note` records quiet bus activity that refreshes
that limit without posting to Slack or Matrix. Use a quiet note before the
configured limit expires if needed. Standalone connectors have no silence
timeout. Keep this session maintenance separate from conversation updates.
